R(id="r5",time="21:00",theme="dark",tag="MARKET WATCH",area="CANADA",fmt="market watch news (all six big banks expect a Dec. 9 hike)",aud="buyer",series="market-watch",mood="tense",
 hook="All six banks: *Dec. 9 hike?",kick="ALL SIX AGREE",hprop="chart",hsay="Six big banks expect a December ninth hike.",
 clip={"source":"Bloomberg","date":"Nov 26, 2026","headline":"Every major Canadian bank now calls for a *December hike"},
 news=("Bloomberg","Nov 26, 2026","All six big banks now expect a hike.","Bloomberg reports all six big Canadian banks now expect the Bank of Canada to hike on December ninth."),
 pts=[("Where rates are","chart","Rate is 2.5% after October's hike. Swaps price about 80% odds.","First, the rate is two point five percent after October's hike, and swap traders put the odds of a December hike at about eighty percent.","Bloomberg, Nov 26, 2026. Odds as of Nov 25."),
      ("Per $1M borrowed","calculator","A quarter-point move could add about $139 a month per $1M.","Now the twist. Next, on a one million dollar mortgage, a quarter point could add about one hundred thirty-nine dollars a month.","Example: 4.34%, 25 years, semi-annual compounding."),
      ("Who it touches","handshake","Variable rates follow the Bank directly. Fixed follow bond yields.","Finally, variable rates tend to follow the Bank of Canada directly, while fixed rates follow bond yields, so check which one you have.","Forecasts can change. Not financial or mortgage advice.")],
 cta="Like, comment and *share with an owner renewing soon",csay="Like, comment and share this with someone renewing soon. "+CTAW,
 fine="Forecasts can change. Not financial or mortgage advice.",
 cap="All six banks: Dec. 9 hike?\n\nBloomberg reported on November 26, 2026 that all six of Canada's biggest banks now expect the Bank of Canada to raise its rate at the December 9 decision. BMO and CIBC moved their forecasts from January to December earlier this week, and TD was the last to switch. The Bank's rate is 2.5% after its October hike, the first increase in three years, and traders in overnight swaps put the odds of a December hike at about 80% as of November 25 (Bloomberg). Global News reported (November 25-26) that CIBC's Avery Shenfeld said December \"might be where the rate hikes stop,\" while BMO's Sal Guatieri cautioned a hike \"could end up being premature\" if the economy softens.\nWhat it could mean: variable-rate borrowers could see a change in their rate or in how much of their payment goes to interest, while fixed rates follow bond yields. Example per $1,000,000 borrowed: at an example 4.34% (25-year amortization, interest compounded semi-annually), the monthly mortgage is about $5,446; at 4.59% (a quarter point higher) it is about $5,585, roughly $139 more. Calculations by Krishna Rander's team. Forecasts can change, and these are the banks' and traders' expectations, not a forecast from me.\nRaise, hold or cut? Comment your guess.\n\nBuying or renewing? "+INFO+"\n\nForecasts can change. Not financial or mortgage advice.\nSources: Bloomberg via Yahoo Finance, \"All of Canada's Big Six Banks Now See December Rate Hike\" (Nov 26, 2026); Global News, \"Bank of Canada expected to hike interest rate next month, economists say\" (Nov 25-26, 2026); Ratehub.ca (4.34% example rate, Nov 26, 2026). Checked Nov 28, 2026.\n\n"+GEN,
 tags="#bankofcanada #mortgagerates #GTArealestate #keyswithkrishna",
 pattern="5 timing + countdown to a dated event",trigger="tension before a dated decision, surprise at the per-$1M figure",share="a friend with a mortgage renewal coming"),
